    "id": "prop:static-nonreduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "The sequential target is not a one-normalizer static exponential tilt on \\(\\mathcal P_T\\): at \\(T=2\\), with constant covariates, fair \\(A_1\\), deterministic \\(A_2=A_1\\), \\(Y=A_2\\), and \\(c=\\sqrt2\\log(3/2)\\), the sequential functional equals \\(3/5\\), whereas tilting the observed law once by \\(\\delta^{A_1+A_2}\\) and globally normalizing yields \\(9/13\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(T\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal P_T\\)",
      "\\(X_t\\)",
      "\\(A_t\\)",
      "\\(Y\\)",
      "\\(c\\)",
      "\\(\\delta\\)",
      "\\(\\psi_T(P,c)\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:model-class",
      "def:estimand",
      "prop:identification"
    ],
    "justification": "The counterexample blocks a tempting flattening that would erase the nested-prefix learning problem.",
    "gap": "SchindlShenKennedy2024 and HuangDongLinAntonelli2026 analyze static single-normalizer tilts; their target algebra cannot be substituted for the sequential functional.",
    "consumer": "The lower-bound and estimator proofs must preserve the original longitudinal intervention rather than import a static rate verbatim.",
    "proof_tex": "Take \\(T=2\\), let both covariates be identically zero, let \\(A_1\\) be fair, set \\(A_2=A_1\\), and set \\(Y=A_2\\).  A compatible full-data construction is \\(Y^{(a_1,a_2)}=a_2\\); hence consistency holds, and sequential exchangeability holds because every potential outcome is constant.  Thus this law belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_2\\).  With \\(c=\\sqrt2\\log(3/2)\\), one has \\(\\eta=\\log(3/2)\\) and \\(\\delta=3/2\\).  The first action is fair, so its incremental probability of one is\n\\[\n q_1^P(1)=\\frac{\\delta/2}{1+(\\delta-1)/2}=\\frac{\\delta}{1+\\delta}=\\frac35.\n\\]\nThe second action is deterministic conditional on \\(H_2\\), and the incremental transformation fixes probabilities zero and one.  Consequently \\(A_2=A_1\\) also under \\(Q_{P,c}\\), whence\n\\[\n \\psi_2(P,c)=E_{Q_{P,c}}[A_2]=\\frac35.\n\\]\nBy contrast, a single static tilt of the two observed trajectories \\((A_1,A_2)=(0,0)\\) and \\((1,1)\\) assigns relative masses \\(1\\) and \\(\\delta^2\\).  Its terminal mean is therefore\n\\[\n \\frac{\\delta^2}{1+\\delta^2}=\\frac{9/4}{1+9/4}=\\frac9{13}\\neq\\frac35.\n\\]\nThis explicit member of the stated class proves nonreduction; no alternative statistical target has been introduced.",
